ategy on nearly identical data (tencent's ~2-year window). Run 2 is the current state of run_dir, and the artifacts are internally consistent. The metrics I should report are those from the final run (Run 2), which are the artifacts currently present in run_dir:\n\nRun 2 metrics (verbatim from the backtest tool output):\n- final_value: 578,100.81\n- total_return: -0.4219 (-42.19%)\n- annual_return: -0.2413 (-24.13% annualized)\n- max_drawdown: -0.4570 (-45.70%)\n- sharpe: -1.8462\n- sortino: -1.6609, calmar: -0.5281\n- win_rate: 16.67%\n- trade_count: 18\n- benchmark_return: -5.52% (buy-and-hold benchmark)\n- excess_return: -36.67%\n- avg_holding_days: 11.1\n- max_consecutive_loss: 6\n- position_adjustment: hold, rebalance_tolerance 0.05, dropped adjustments: 0\n- risk_xray_avg_invested: 40.03%\n\nWait, but there's one issue: the annual_return here is computed over the period starting from the config's start_date 2022-10-01, but the equity actually starts on 2022-12-09 (the first data bar). Either way, it's what the tool computed. I'll report it verbatim and add a note about the period.\n\nAlso, the annualized return denominator: from 2022-10-01 to 2024-12-31 = 2.247 years. final 578,100.81 / 1,000,000 → (0.578)^(1/2.247)-1 = ln(0.578)=-0.548; /2.247=-0.2439; e^-0.2439-1 = -21.7%… the tool says -24.13%. Maybe it uses the equity curve's start (2022-12-09) → 2.06 years: -0.548/2.06=-0.266 → e^-0.266-1=-23.4%. Close but doesn't match exactly; the tool might use a different convention (e.g., 252-day-based). Not important — I'll report verbatim.\n\nThe user asked for the metrics over the period 2023-01-01 → 2024-12-31. The backtest period actually measured spans from data start (2022-12-09) through 2024-12-31, and the strategy was flat until the first golden cross on 2023-01-09 (no fills in 2022 — verified in both runs' trade logs; Run 2's first fill is also 2023-01-09). So equity from 2022-12-09 to 2023-01-09 was flat at 1,000,000 — the inclusion of the warmup period has almost no impact on returns (flat, no fees); only the annualization denominator differs slightly. Good, worth noting.\n\nWarmup situation: the data starts 2022-12-09 → SMA20 needs 20 bars → first valid around 2023-01-05 (16 bars in December + 4 in January). So the MA is not valid from the very first trading day of 2023, but becomes valid about 3–4 trading days into 2023. The first trade on 2023-01-09 (golden cross) is only 2–3 bars after MA validity. The user's ideal warmup (fully valid MA on 2023-01-01) could not be achieved, because the only data source that worked (tencent) caps at about 2 years of history, and akshare failed with a network error. I tried both (\"auto\"→tencent, \"akshare\"→failed→tencent fallback). I should honestly note this caveat: about the first 3 bars of 2023 are MA warmup (flat), and the first actionable cross was captured on 2023-01-09.\n\nHmm, should I try baostock? The user mentioned \"baostock\" as a possible source, but the list of sources supported by the engine in the skill docs is: auto / tushare / yfinance / okx / akshare / ccxt. baostock is not in the supported list. The tool description lists \"yfinance\", \"okx\", \"tushare\", \"akshare\", \"ccxt\", \"auto\" as supported sources. So baostock is not supported by this engine. tushare requires a token (per the user, unavailable). yfinance for 600519.SS is possible, but… the engine auto-routes .SH codes to tushare/akshare-family sources; forcing \"yfinance\" on an A-share code — hmm. Would yfinance with \"600519.SS\" be able to fetch full history? Probably, but the engine's yfinance adapter expects .US/.HK-style codes and the mapping 600519.SH → 600519.SS is uncertain. That's another speculative attempt; per budget discipline, stop and report with caveats. The tencent data covers the entire evaluation window 2023-01-01→2024-12-31 (verified: bars on 2024-12-31 and 2023-01-09… well, I've verified the tail end 2024-12-25..31 and the data start 2022-12-09; continuous coverage is implied by the 500 rows ≈ full trading calendar).\n\nSanity check on coverage completeness: 500 data rows. Trading days from 2022-12-09 to 2024-12-31: about 16 in Dec 2022 + 242 in 2023 + 243 in 2024 ≈ 501. ✓ So coverage of the requested evaluation window is complete.\n\nCosts: commission 0.1% per side (the engine's default, explicitly set in the config); no separate slippage parameter exists in the engine's config schema — the execution model is next-bar… actually, fills happen at the price shown on the signal bar (2023-01-09 buy @ 1656.47 — probably that bar's close or open; not specified, won't overclaim). I'll write it as: one-way commission 0.1%, engine default execution (no additional slippage parameter in the schema).\n\nAnother consideration — which run's numbers should I report? The current artifacts in run_dir are Run 2's (config's source=akshare, data_sources=tencent, start 2022-10-01). Run 1's artifacts were overwritten. So the workspace state = Run 2. I'll report Run 2's numbers verbatim.\n\nAlso noting: benchmark_return -5.52% = buy-and-hold of 600519 over the same window (engine's default benchmark), useful context.\n\nOK, final answer. Structure per output contract:\n1. Result — headline metrics with data window\n2. Artifacts — paths\n3. Caveats — warmup shortfall, akshare failure, no OOS validation\n\nAlso the output format the user requested: strategy summary (2–3 sentences), data source used, metrics block, run_dir path.\n\nTrade count: 18 round-trip trades (35 fills + 1 final close… actually rebalance_count=35; 37 rows in trades.csv = 18 buys + 19 sells? Let me count: rows 2–37 = 36 rows → 18 buys, 18 sells. Hmm, 37 rows minus the header = 36 rows = 18 pairs. trade_count=18 = 18 round-trips. Good. The final sell on 2024-12-31 is marked end_of_backtest.\n\nSharpe ratio: -1.8462 (verbatim). Max DD: -45.70%. Total return -42.19%, annualized -24.13%.\n\nThis strategy simply performed badly — Moutai fell from about 1700 to about 1440 over 2023–2024, and the SMA5/20 crossover was whipsawed repeatedly (win rate 16.7%, 6 consecutive losses). Honest negative result. Benchmark buy-and-hold was -5.5%; the strategy underperformed by about 36.7pp. That's the truth from the data — I'll report it.\n\nCaveats to include:\n- akshare connection failed (RemoteDisconnected); the engine silently fell back to tencent (recorded in run_card's data_sources=[\"tencent\"]).\n- tencent only returned about 2 years: data starts 2022-12-09 instead of the requested warmup start 2022-10-01 → SMA20 only becomes valid about 4 trading days into January 2023 (first trade 2023-01-09); the position is flat during warmup, so no signal distortion, but the MA was not strictly valid from the first trading day of 2023.\n- Metrics span the data start 2022-12-09 → 2024-12-31; equity was flat (no trades, no costs) until the first golden cross on 2023-01-09.\n- Commission 0.1% per side, no additional slippage configured; execution on signal bar.\n- No out-of-sample split / statistical validation run (the validation field is null).\n- tushare/baostock unavailable (no token / not supported by the engine).\n\nArtifacts: config.json, code/signal_engine.py, run_card.json/.md, artifacts/{equity,metrics,trades,positions,target_positions,ohlcv_600519.SH}.csv, fills.jsonl, risk_xray, rebalance_notes.\n\nrun_dir: /private/tmp/d_l2_smoke/runs/dual_ma_600519\nAll verified. Here is the complete report.\n\n## Result\n\n**Strategy summary:** SMA(5)/SMA(20) close-price crossover on Kweichow Moutai (600519.SH), long-only, full position on golden cross, flat on death cross, daily bars, measured over 2022-12-09 → 2024-12-31 (data start; equity was flat with zero trades until the first golden cross on 2023-01-09, so all economic activity falls inside the requested 2023–2024 window). The strategy lost money decisively: Moutai trended down for most of 2023–2024 and the fast crossover was whipsawed repeatedly — 18 round trips with only a 16.7% win rate and 6 consecutive losers, un